Carafe Lab: under NCE code the guest maps at run time is patched too. Carafe's loader maps the runtime itself
with svcMapProcessCodeMemory and makes its text executable with svcSetProcessMemoryPermission; Eden patched
only modules it loaded at boot, so the runtime's svc instructions would have reached the host kernel. When an
executable range starts with an NRO header, its supervisor calls and system register accesses go through a
patch section placed in free address space within branch range; the section goes away with the mapping. Other
executable mappings, such as x86 images and Wine's PE modules, are left as they are. The return points are
looked up under a lock. Patch sections of boot modules are made inaccessible to the guest before they are
made executable on the host, so the host mapping stays executable.
GPL-3.0-or-later, as the files it changes.

